Falsifies `capability::verification::subset_is_transitive`.

The defect: the subset test tolerates one right's worth of slack,
`(self.0 & !other.0).count_ones() <= 1` in place of an exact `== 0`.

**The defect is chosen to discriminate, and that is the point of this record.** The obvious
mutation here is the dropped `!` that `verification.subset_is_reflexive.patch` uses, and it does
turn this harness red too, which would have recorded nothing the reflexive patch does not
already record. These harnesses are the clearest candidates for proving trivia,
so the useful question is whether transitivity catches anything its neighbours do not. This
defect answers it: the relation above stays **reflexive** (`a & !a` is zero, and zero bits is
not more than one), so `subset_is_reflexive` stays green, and only transitivity sees it.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(a.is_subset_of(c))`, at `a = 0b11`, `b = 0b01`,
`c = 0b00`, where each link of the chain gives away one right and the chain gives away two. That
is exactly the laundering the doc comment says a flat subset check is safe because it cannot do.

diff --git a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
@@ -142,3 +142,3 @@ impl Rights {
     pub const fn is_subset_of(self, other: Rights) -> bool {
-        self.0 & !other.0 == 0
+        (self.0 & !other.0).count_ones() <= 1
     }
